module

public import Mathlib.RingTheory.LocalProperties.Reduced
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.LocalRing.ResidueField.Fiber
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.RingHom.Flat
public import StacksFrontier.RingTheory.Henselian.HenselizationMinimalPrimes
public import StacksFrontier.RingTheory.MinimalPrimes.Reduced

/-!
# The localization of a henselization at a branch, over the localization at the base

Let `A` be a local ring and let `B` be a henselization or a strict henselization of `A`. A
*branch* of `A` is a minimal prime `q` of `B`; it lies over a minimal prime `p` of `A`
(`IsLocalRing.mem_minimalPrimes_iff_liesOver_minimalPrime_of_isHenselization`). This file
compares the two localizations `A_p` and `B_q`: the induced local homomorphism `A_p ⟶ B_q` is
flat and **unramified**, in the sense that it carries the maximal ideal of `A_p` onto the
maximal ideal of `B_q`.

The second statement is the one with content. For an arbitrary flat algebra it is false; what
makes it true here is that the fibre `κ(p) ⊗_A B` of an ind-étale algebra is *reduced*
(`StacksDraft.fiber_reduced_and_krullDimLE_zero`). Since `q` is a minimal prime, the maximal
ideal of `B_q` consists of nilpotents, and a nilpotent which survives in a reduced quotient is
zero, so every element of the maximal ideal of `B_q` already lies in the ideal generated by
`p`.

Taken together with a length identity along a flat unramified local homomorphism, this says
that `B_q` and `A_p` have the same length over themselves: the multiplicity of a branch of `A`
is the multiplicity of the component of `Spec A` it lies on. That conclusion is
`Ring.length_localizationAtPrime_eq_of_isHenselization`, which lives in
`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Spaces.Chow.ComponentMultiplicityEtaleInvariance` beside the
length engine it uses.

## Main results

* `StacksDraft.isReduced_quotient_map_localizationAtPrime_of_isReduced_fiber`: if the fibre
  `κ(p) ⊗_R S` is reduced then so is `S_q ⧸ p S_q` for every prime `q` of `S` over `p`.
* `StacksDraft.map_maximalIdeal_localRingHom_of_mem_minimalPrimes_of_isReduced_fiber`: with a
  reduced fibre and `q` a *minimal* prime, `p A_p` extends to the maximal ideal of `S_q`.
* `IsLocalRing.map_maximalIdeal_localRingHom_of_isHenselization`: the instance of that for a
  henselization or strict henselization, whose fibres are reduced because it is ind-étale.
* `IsLocalRing.flat_localRingHom_of_isHenselization`: and the induced homomorphism of
  localizations is flat.

## Implementation notes

The reducedness statement is the ring-theoretic content of the two existing scheme-level
lemmas named `isReduced_quotient_localization_of_isReduced_fiber`
(`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.MoreMorphisms.ReducedFibre.ProperOverDvr`, and a `private`
copy in `StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Morphisms.Fibres.ReducedOverValuationRing`), both of
which take reducedness of a *scheme* fibre of a map out of a discrete valuation ring and
produce the quotient by a uniformizer. Neither is usable here: the hypothesis is about
`Scheme.Hom.fiber` and the conclusion is about a principal ideal. The proof below is theirs,
with `Ideal.Fiber.algEquivAux₂` doing the work. The transport along `q = qbar ∩ S` is carried
out on `PrimeSpectrum S` rather than on `Ideal S`, so that the primality instance is bundled
with the ideal and the two can be substituted together.

The fibre hypothesis is stated as `IsReduced (p.Fiber S)` rather than as ind-étaleness so that
the two statements separate: the first is pure commutative algebra about a flat algebra with
reduced fibres, and only the henselization corollaries need the ind-étale input.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 00EU](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00EU)
